        {
            "cardtype": "basic",
            "tags": "emd101,week1,wordparts",
            "content": {
                "front": "<p>What does the suffix <strong>-itis</strong> mean?</p>",
                "frontformat": 1,
                "back": "<p><strong>Inflammation.</strong> Example: hepat<em>itis</em> — inflammation of the liver.</p>",
                "backformat": 1
            }
        },
        {
            "cardtype": "basic",
            "tags": "emd101,week1,wordparts",
            "content": {
                "front": "<p>What is a <strong>combining vowel</strong> and why is it used?</p>",
                "frontformat": 1,
                "back": "<p>A vowel — usually <strong>o</strong> — placed between word parts to make a term easier to pronounce, as in gastr<em>o</em>enterology. It carries no meaning of its own.</p>",
                "backformat": 1
            }
        },
        {
            "cardtype": "basic",
            "tags": "emd101,week1,wordparts",
            "content": {
                "front": "<p>What does a <strong>prefix</strong> do in a medical term?</p>",
                "frontformat": 1,
                "back": "<p>It sits at the <strong>beginning</strong> of the term and modifies its meaning — often indicating time, speed, location, number or absence (hyper-, brady-, sub-).</p>",
                "backformat": 1
            }
        },
        {
            "cardtype": "basic",
            "tags": "emd101,week1,wordparts",
            "content": {
                "front": "<p>What does a <strong>suffix</strong> do in a medical term?</p>",
                "frontformat": 1,
                "back": "<p>It sits at the <strong>end</strong> of the term and usually names a condition, procedure or specialty (-itis, -logy, -gram).</p>",
                "backformat": 1
            }
        }
    ]
